1
00:00:00,119 --> 00:00:04,840
noticed that the milk had disappeared.

2
00:00:04,840 --> 00:00:07,800
Chapter 3.

3
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:11,400
How they toiled and sweated to get the hay in.

4
00:00:11,400 --> 00:00:18,440
But their efforts were rewarded, for the harvest was an even bigger success than they had hoped.

5
00:00:18,440 --> 00:00:22,840
Sometimes the work was hard. The implements had been designed for human

6
00:00:22,840 --> 00:00:25,519
beings and not for animals.

7
00:00:25,519 --> 00:00:30,440
And it was a great drawback that no animal was able to use any tool that

8
00:00:30,440 --> 00:00:33,639
involved standing on his hind legs.

9
00:00:33,639 --> 00:00:39,360
But the pigs were so clever that they could think of a way round every difficulty.

10
00:00:39,360 --> 00:00:44,840
As for the horses, they knew every inch of the field, and in fact, understood

11
00:00:44,840 --> 00:00:51,159
the business of mowing and raking far better than Jones and his men had ever done.

12
00:00:51,159 --> 00:00:57,840
The pigs did not actually work, but directed and supervised the others.

13
00:00:57,840 --> 00:01:03,159
With their superior knowledge, it was natural that they should assume the leadership.

14
00:01:03,159 --> 00:01:07,320
Boxer and Clover would harness themselves to the cutter or the horse

15
00:01:07,320 --> 00:01:14,800
rake. No bits or reins were needed in these days, of course, and steadily round and round the

16
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:20,880
field with a pig walking behind and calling out, "Gee up, comrade."

17
00:01:20,880 --> 00:01:24,320
or "Whoa back, comrade."

18
00:01:24,320 --> 00:01:26,519
as the case might be.

19
00:01:26,519 --> 00:01:32,719
And every animal, down to the humblest, worked at turning the hay and gathering it.

20
00:01:32,719 --> 00:01:38,719
Even the ducks and hens toiled to and fro all day in the sun, carrying tiny

21
00:01:38,719 --> 00:01:42,039
wisps of hay in their beaks.

22
00:01:42,039 --> 00:01:46,920
In the end, they finished the harvest in two days less time than it had usually

23
00:01:46,920 --> 00:01:49,360
taken Jones and his men.

24
00:01:49,360 --> 00:01:54,559
Moreover, it was the the harvest that the farm had ever seen.

25
00:01:54,559 --> 00:01:56,840
There was no wastage whatever.

26
00:01:56,840 --> 00:02:03,400
The hens and ducks, with their sharp eyes, had gathered up the very last stalk.

27
00:02:03,400 --> 00:02:08,400
And not an animal on the farm had stolen so much as a mouthful.

28
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:13,199
All through that summer, the work of the farm went like clockwork.

29
00:02:13,199 --> 00:02:18,360
The animals were happy as they had never conceived it possible to be.

30
00:02:18,360 --> 00:02:24,360
Every mouthful of food was an acute, positive pleasure now that it was truly

31
00:02:24,360 --> 00:02:31,920
their own food, produced by themselves and for themselves, not doled out to them by a grudging

32
00:02:31,920 --> 00:02:33,639
master.

33
00:02:33,639 --> 00:02:40,639
With the worthless, parasitical human beings gone, there was more for everyone to eat.

34
00:02:40,639 --> 00:02:42,599
There was more leisure, too.

35
00:02:42,599 --> 00:02:47,880
Inexperienced though the animals were, they met with many difficulties. For

36
00:02:47,880 --> 00:02:52,599
instance, later in the year, when they harvested the corn, they had to tread it

37
00:02:52,599 --> 00:02:58,000
out in the ancient style and blow away the chaff with their breath, since the

38
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:01,119
farm possessed no threshing machine.

39
00:03:01,119 --> 00:03:06,960
But the pigs, with their cleverness, and Boxer, with his tremendous muscles,

40
00:03:06,960 --> 00:03:09,800
always pulled them through.

41
00:03:09,800 --> 00:03:13,679
Boxer was the admiration of everybody.

42
00:03:13,679 --> 00:03:20,639
He had been a hard worker even in Jones's time, but now he seemed more like three horses

43
00:03:20,639 --> 00:03:22,159
than one.

44
00:03:22,159 --> 00:03:28,920
There were days when the entire work of the farm seemed to rest upon his mighty shoulders.

45
00:03:28,920 --> 00:03:34,239
From morning to night, he was pushing and pulling, always at the spot where

46
00:03:34,239 --> 00:03:36,519
the work was hardest.

47
00:03:36,519 --> 00:03:40,760
He had made an arrangement with one of the cockerels to call him in the

48
00:03:40,760 --> 00:03:45,800
mornings half an hour earlier than anyone else, and would put in some

49
00:03:45,800 --> 00:03:53,159
volunteer labor at whatever seemed to be most needed before the regular day's work began.

50
00:03:53,159 --> 00:04:00,360
His answer to every problem, every setback was, >> I will work harder.

51
00:04:00,360 --> 00:04:03,880
>> Which he had adopted as his personal motto.

52
00:04:03,880 --> 00:04:07,199
But everyone worked according to his capacity.

53
00:04:07,199 --> 00:04:12,920
The hens and ducks, for instance, saved five bushels of corn at the harvest by

54
00:04:12,920 --> 00:04:15,800
gathering up the stray grains.

55
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:22,079
Nobody stole, nobody grumbled over his rations. The quarreling and biting and

56
00:04:22,079 --> 00:04:28,599
jealousy, which had been normal features of life in the old days, had almost disappeared.

57
00:04:28,599 --> 00:04:32,800
Nobody shirked, or almost nobody.

58
00:04:32,800 --> 00:04:37,480
Mollie, it was true, was not good at getting up in the mornings and had a way

59
00:04:37,480 --> 00:04:42,760
of leaving work early on the ground that there was a stone in her hoof.

60
00:04:42,760 --> 00:04:47,159
And the behavior of the cat was somewhat peculiar.

61
00:04:47,159 --> 00:04:53,239
It was soon noticed that when there was work to be done, the cat could never be found.

62
00:04:53,239 --> 00:04:58,760
She would vanish for hours on end and then reappear at meal times or in the

63
00:04:58,760 --> 00:05:03,320
evening after work was over as though nothing had happened.

64
00:05:03,320 --> 00:05:09,159
But she always made such excellent excuses and purred so affectionately

65
00:05:09,159 --> 00:05:13,639
that it was impossible not to believe in her good intentions.

66
00:05:13,639 --> 00:05:19,280
Old Benjamin, the donkey, seemed quite unchanged since the rebellion.

67
00:05:19,280 --> 00:05:24,079
He did his work in the same slow, obstinate way as he had done it in

68
00:05:24,079 --> 00:05:31,000
Jones's time, never shirking and never volunteering for extra work, either.

69
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:36,480
About the rebellion and its results, he would express no opinion.

70
00:05:36,480 --> 00:05:42,440
When asked whether he was not happier now that Jones was gone, he would say only,

71
00:05:42,440 --> 00:05:44,440
>> Donkeys live a long time.

72
00:05:44,440 --> 00:05:47,360
None of you has ever seen a dead donkey.

73
00:05:47,360 --> 00:05:51,679
And the others had to be content with this cryptic answer.

74
00:05:51,679 --> 00:05:56,840
On Sundays, there was no work. Breakfast was an hour later than usual, and after

75
00:05:56,840 --> 00:06:02,360
breakfast, there was a ceremony, which was observed every week without fail.

76
00:06:02,360 --> 00:06:05,199
First came the hoisting of the flag.

77
00:06:05,199 --> 00:06:10,760
Snowball had found in the harness room an old green tablecloth of Mrs. Jones's

78
00:06:10,760 --> 00:06:15,079
and had painted on it a hoof and a horn in white.

79
00:06:15,079 --> 00:06:20,039
This was run up the flagstaff in the farmhouse garden every Sunday morning.

80
00:06:20,039 --> 00:06:25,039
The flag was green, Snowball explained, to represent the green fields of

81
00:06:25,039 --> 00:06:30,360
England, while the hoof and horn signified the future Republic of the

82
00:06:30,360 --> 00:06:36,679
animals, which would arise when the human race had been finally overthrown.

83
00:06:36,679 --> 00:06:41,360
After the hoisting of the flag, all the animals trooped into the big barn for a

84
00:06:41,360 --> 00:06:45,440
general assembly, which was known as the meeting.

85
00:06:45,440 --> 00:06:52,239
Here, the work of the coming week was planned out, and resolutions were put forward and debated.

86
00:06:52,239 --> 00:06:56,039
It was always the pigs who put forward the resolutions.

87
00:06:56,039 --> 00:07:02,719
The other animals understood how to vote, but could never think of any resolutions of their own.

88
00:07:02,719 --> 00:07:08,280
Snowball and Napoleon were by far the most active in the debates.

89
00:07:08,280 --> 00:07:13,400
But it was noticed that these two were never in agreement. Whatever suggestion

90
00:07:13,400 --> 00:07:18,280
either of them made, the other could be counted on to oppose it.

91
00:07:18,280 --> 00:07:23,360
Even when it was resolved, a thing no one could object to in itself, to set

92
00:07:23,360 --> 00:07:27,800
aside the small paddock behind the orchard as a home of rest for animals

93
00:07:27,800 --> 00:07:33,280
who were past work, there was a stormy debate over the correct retiring age for

94
00:07:33,280 --> 00:07:36,039
each class of animal.

95
00:07:36,039 --> 00:07:41,000
The meeting always ended with the singing of Beasts of England, and the

96
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:44,239
afternoon was given up to recreation.

97
00:07:44,239 --> 00:07:48,760
The pigs had set aside the harness room as a headquarters for themselves. Here,

98
00:07:48,760 --> 00:07:52,800
in the evenings, they studied blacksmithing, carpentering, and other

99
00:07:52,800 --> 00:07:57,320
necessary arts from books which they had brought out of the farmhouse.

100
00:07:57,320 --> 00:08:01,719
Snowball also busied himself with organizing the other animals into what

101
00:08:01,719 --> 00:08:04,480
he called animal committees.

102
00:08:04,480 --> 00:08:06,880
He was indefatigable at this.

103
00:08:06,880 --> 00:08:11,079
He formed the egg production committee for the hens, the Clean Tails League for

104
00:08:11,079 --> 00:08:15,159
the cows, the Wild Comrades Re-education Committee.

105
00:08:15,159 --> 00:08:21,320
The object of this was to tame the rats and rabbits, the Whiter Wool Movement for the sheep,

106
00:08:21,320 --> 00:08:22,880
and various others.

107
00:08:22,880 --> 00:08:29,880
Besides instituting classes in reading and writing, on the whole, these projects were a failure.

108
00:08:29,880 --> 00:08:35,119
The attempt to tame the wild creatures, for instance, broke down almost immediately.

109
00:08:35,119 --> 00:08:38,800
They continued to behave very much as before, and when treated with

110
00:08:38,800 --> 00:08:42,159
generosity, simply took advantage of it.

111
00:08:42,159 --> 00:08:47,440
The cat joined the Re-education Committee and was very active in it for some days.

112
00:08:47,440 --> 00:08:51,679
She was seen one day sitting on a roof and talking to some sparrows who were

113
00:08:51,679 --> 00:08:53,760
just out of her reach.

114
00:08:53,760 --> 00:08:58,480
She was telling them that all animals were now comrades and that any sparrow

115
00:08:58,480 --> 00:09:02,119
who chose could come and perch on her paw.

116
00:09:02,119 --> 00:09:04,719
But the sparrows kept their distance.

117
00:09:04,719 --> 00:09:09,199
The reading and writing classes, however, were a great success.

118
00:09:09,199 --> 00:09:14,800
By the autumn, almost every animal on the farm was literate in some degree.

119
00:09:14,800 --> 00:09:19,800
As for the pigs, they could already read and write perfectly.

120
00:09:19,800 --> 00:09:24,239
The dogs learned to read fairly well, but were not interested in reading

121
00:09:24,239 --> 00:09:27,840
anything except the Seven Commandments.

122
00:09:27,840 --> 00:09:32,960
Muriel, the goat, could read somewhat better than the dogs, and sometimes used

123
00:09:32,960 --> 00:09:37,199
to read to the others in the evenings from scraps of newspaper which she found

124
00:09:37,199 --> 00:09:39,360
on the rubbish heap.

125
00:09:39,360 --> 00:09:45,039
Benjamin could read as well as any pig, but never exercised his faculty.

126
00:09:45,039 --> 00:09:49,519
So far as he knew, he said, there was nothing worth reading.

127
00:09:49,519 --> 00:09:54,719
Clover learned the whole alphabet but could not put words together.

128
00:09:54,719 --> 00:09:57,880
Boxer could not get beyond the letter D.

129
00:09:57,880 --> 00:10:04,840
He would trace out A, B, C, D in the dust with his great hoof, and then would

130
00:10:04,840 --> 00:10:11,519
stand staring at the letters with his ears back, sometimes shaking his forelock, trying

131
00:10:11,519 --> 00:10:17,719
with all his might to remember what came next and never succeeding.

132
00:10:17,719 --> 00:10:24,000
On several occasions indeed, he did learn E, F, G, H,

133
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:28,280
but by the time he knew them, it was always discovered that he had forgotten

134
00:10:28,280 --> 00:10:32,280
A, B, C, and D.

135
00:10:32,280 --> 00:10:37,239
Finally, he decided to be content with the first four letters and used to write

136
00:10:37,239 --> 00:10:41,880
them out once or twice every day to refresh his memory.

137
00:10:41,880 --> 00:10:48,679
Mollie refused to learn any but the six letters which spelt her own name.

138
00:10:48,679 --> 00:10:53,599
She would form these very neatly out of pieces of twig and would then decorate

139
00:10:53,599 --> 00:10:58,119
them with a flower or two and walk round them admiring them.

140
00:10:58,119 --> 00:11:02,719
None of the other animals on the farm could get further than the letter A.

141
00:11:02,719 --> 00:11:07,400
It was also found that the stupider animals, such as the sheep, hens, and

142
00:11:07,400 --> 00:11:12,119
ducks, were unable to learn the seven commandments by heart.

143
00:11:12,119 --> 00:11:16,599
After much thought, Snowball declared that the seven commandments could, in

144
00:11:16,599 --> 00:11:22,000
effect, be reduced to a single maxim, namely four legs good, two legs bad.
